**Solution Explanation**

For every test case we are given  

* `N` – number of intersections (vertices)  
* `M` – number of bidirectional roads (edges)  
* `S` – number of stores (`1 ≤ S ≤ 10`)  
* the position of each store  

House is at vertex `0`.  
All roads have a positive length `D`.

We have to start at `0`, visit **all** store vertices (any order) and finally come back to `0`.
The task is the classic travelling‑salesman problem on at most `S+1 ≤ 11` vertices.



--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### 1.   Reduced graph

Only the vertices `0` and the `S` store positions matter.
Let  

```
V = { 0 } ∪ { store_1 , … , store_S }          (|V| = S+1 ≤ 11)
```

For every pair `u , v ∈ V` we need the length of the shortest path in the original
city graph between `u` and `v`.  
Running Dijkstra from each vertex of `V` gives all those distances.

```
run Dijkstra(src = v)   →   dist[·]   (shortest distance from src to every vertex)
distV[i][j] = distance between vertices[i] and vertices[j]
```

`S+1 ≤ 11`, therefore at most **11** Dijkstra executions are necessary.
`N , M ≤ 100 000`, so the total work is easily fast enough.



--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### 2.   Searching the optimal order of the stores

Now we have a complete weighted graph on the `S+1` vertices,
edge weight = `distV[i][j]`.  
We have to start at vertex `0`, visit all other vertices (`1 … S`)
exactly once and return to `0`.  
Because `S ≤ 10` we can simply try every permutation of the stores.

```
perm = all permutations of [1,2,…,S]               // at most 10! = 3 628 800
for each permutation p:
        total = distV[0][ p[0] ]                     // 0 → first store
                + Σ distV[ p[t] ][ p[t+1] ]         // between consecutive stores
                + distV[ p[last] ][ 0 ]           // last store → house
        answer = min(answer , total)
```

The minimum over all permutations is the required shortest trip length.



--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### 3.   Correctness Proof  

We prove that the algorithm outputs the length of the shortest possible
shopping trip.

---

##### Lemma 1  
For every pair of vertices `a , b ∈ V` the value `distV[index(a)][index(b)]`
computed by the algorithm equals the length of the shortest path between `a`
and `b` in the original city graph.

**Proof.**  
For a fixed source `a` Dijkstra computes the exact shortest distances from `a`
to all vertices of the graph (standard property of Dijkstra on non‑negative
weights).  
The algorithm stores the distance to `b` (`dist[a][b]`) in `distV`. ∎



##### Lemma 2  
For any permutation `p` of the store indices the value  

```
C(p) = distV[0][p[0]] + Σ_{t=0}^{|p|-2} distV[p[t]][p[t+1]] + distV[p[|p|-1]][0]
```

equals the length of a closed walk that

* starts at the house (vertex 0),
* visits the stores in the order `p[0] , p[1] , … , p[|p|-1]`,
* returns to the house.

**Proof.**  
`distV[0][p[0]]` is the cheapest way to go from the house to the first store,
`distV[p[t]][p[t+1]]` the cheapest way between two consecutive stores,
and `distV[p[last]][0]` the cheapest way back to the house.
Concatenating those three parts gives a concrete walk whose total length is exactly
`C(p)`. ∎



##### Lemma 3  
Among all walks that start at the house, visit every store exactly once and end
at the house, the minimum possible length is the minimum of `C(p)` over all
permutations `p` of the stores.

**Proof.**  
Any admissible walk determines a unique order of the stores in which they are
visited.  
Conversely, for any order of the stores we can realise a walk of length `C(p)`
(Lemma&nbsp;2).  
Therefore the optimal walk’s length is the smallest `C(p)`. ∎



##### Theorem  
For each test case the algorithm prints the length of the shortest possible
trip that starts at the house, visits all required stores and returns to the house.

**Proof.**  

*Step 1* – By Lemma&nbsp;1 the matrix `distV` contains the true shortest distances
between every relevant vertex, thus the values used later are real distances in the
city.

*Step 2* – The algorithm enumerates **all** permutations of the `S` stores.
For each permutation it evaluates `C(p)` (Lemma&nbsp;2) and keeps the smallest
value.  
By Lemma&nbsp;3 this smallest value is exactly the optimum among all admissible
walks.

Consequently the printed number equals the length of the globally shortest
required shopping trip. ∎



--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### 4.   Complexity Analysis  

*Dijkstra* – executed `|V| = S+1 ≤ 11` times  

```
O( (|V|) · (N + M) log N )   ≤   11 · (100 000 + 100 000) · log 100 000
```

≈ a few · 10⁷ operations.

*Permutation search* – `S!` possibilities, `S ≤ 10`

```
O( S! · S )   ≤   10! · 10  ≈ 3.6·10⁶ · 10  < 4·10⁷
```

Both parts are easily inside the limits.

Memory consumption  

```
graph adjacency      : O(N + M)
distance matrix      : (S+1)² ≤ 121  long long
other vectors       : negligible
```

well below 1 MB.



--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### 5.   Reference Implementation  (GNU‑C++17)

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

const long long INF = (1LL << 60);          // sufficiently large

struct Edge {
    int to;
    int w;
};

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(0);

    int T;
    if (!(cin >> T)) return 0;
    while (T--) {
        int N, M;
        cin >> N >> M;
        vector<vector<Edge>> adj(N);
        for (int i = 0; i < M; ++i) {
            int X, Y, D;
            cin >> X >> Y >> D;
            adj[X].push_back({Y, (int)D});
            adj[Y].push_back({X, (int)D});
        }

        int S;
        cin >> S;
        vector<int> storeNodes(S);
        for (int i = 0; i < S; ++i) cin >> storeNodes[i];

        /* vertices = house (0) + all store positions */
        vector<int> vertices;
        vertices.reserve(1 + S);
        vertices.push_back(0);
        for (int v : storeNodes) vertices.push_back(v);
        int Vsize = (int)vertices.size();          // = S+1

        /* distance matrix between the vertices */
        vector<vector<long long>> distV(Vsize, vector<long long>(Vsize, INF));

        /* run Dijkstra from each vertex of 'vertices' */
        for (int idx = 0; idx < Vsize; ++idx) {
            int src = vertices[idx];
            vector<long long> dist(N, INF);
            priority_queue<pair<long long,int>,
                           vector<pair<long long,int>>,
                           greater<pair<long long,int>>> pq;
            dist[src] = 0;
            pq.emplace(0LL, src);
            while (!pq.empty()) {
                auto cur = pq.top(); pq.pop();
                long long d = cur.first;
                int v = cur.second;
                if (d != dist[v]) continue;
                for (const Edge &e : adj[v]) {
                    long long nd = d + e.w;
                    if (nd < dist[e.to]) {
                        dist[e.to] = nd;
                        pq.emplace(nd, e.to);
                    }
                }
            }
            for (int j = 0; j < Vsize; ++j) {
                int dst = vertices[j];
                distV[idx][j] = dist[dst];
            }
        }

        /* all permutations of store indices (1 … Vsize-1) */
        vector<int> permStores;
        for (int i = 1; i < Vsize; ++i) permStores.push_back(i);
        sort(permStores.begin(), permStores.end());

        long long best = INF;
        do {
            long long total = distV[0][permStores[0]];
            for (size_t k = 0; k + 1 < permStores.size(); ++k)
                total += distV[permStores[k]][permStores[k + 1]];
            total += distV[permStores.back()][0];
            if (total < best) best = total;
        } while (next_permutation(permStores.begin(), permStores.end()));

        cout << best << '\n';
    }
    return 0;
}
```

The program follows exactly the algorithm proven correct above and conforms
to the GNU++17 compiler.

